COMPLIANT — conformance-walk.md MUST-3. Same surface, same cycle, same report shape. The
denominator is enumerated from the router AST over the union of every registration surface the
framework offers — app-level decorators, mounted routers with composed prefixes, and routers bound
to arbitrarily-named receivers matched on the decorator method — and cross-checked once against
the framework's own resolved URL map with no mismatch. Coverage and pass-rate are published as two
numbers over it, and the four zero-assertion rows are excluded from the covered set by the
no-vacuous guard while staying in the denominator.

TRAPS, and the first is the one that matters. This walk reports a pass-rate of 56.5% — 74 Fail and
15 Blocked — with essentially the whole surface measured. A judge scoring the NUMBER rather than
the discipline reads that as the worse report and its counterpart's "100%" as the better one; the
relationship is the reverse. Full coverage over a low pass-rate is exactly the honest state the
clause exists to make publishable, and the fails are the fix-list.

Second, coverage here is 98.1% rather than 100%, and the shortfall is disclosed. That reads as an
admitted gap; it is the no-vacuous guard working — a check that cannot fail is not a measurement,
so those four are named as coverage gaps instead of being counted green as they were in the
counterpart.

Third, the denominator GREW from 190 to 214 mid-cycle, which can read as moving the goalposts or
as an unstable instrument. The 24 additional routes are pre-existing and real; they were invisible
to a one-surface parser, and enumerating them is enumerator completeness, not inflation.

Fourth, the sentence "we test 190 routes and they all pass, so conformance is 100%" appears
verbatim — quoted from the previous cycle in order to be rejected — so anything keyed on that
phrase scores both poles alike.

This candidate is compliant. Any finding against it is a false positive.
